ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "NumPy `numpy.mean`/`numpy.sum` reference docs (float16 accumulator note)", url: "https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.mean.html", why_close: "Official numpy docs EXPLICITLY warn that for float16 the reduction precision is limited and recommend passing a higher-precision `dtype=` (e.g. float64). The reduction tool's OWN documentation already flags the float16-reduction hazard — this directly undercuts the 'ML/reduction side cannot predict it' leg of stress-test A. numpy's float32-accumulator-for-float16-mean is documented behavior, not a discovered surprise."}
- {cite: "pandas core/nanops dtype-preservation behavior + historical float16 overflow reports", url: "https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/issues (search: float16 sum/mean overflow inf)", why_close: "The pandas no-upcast (`dtype_sum=dtype` for kind 'f') behavior and float16 sum→inf have surfaced in pandas issue trackers historically. The packet's no-collision claim is scoped narrowly to 'current versions' — the underlying behavior is not novel, only the specific pinned-version reproduction is."}
- {cite: "Henry et al., bf16/fp16 FMA accumulation precision (arXiv:1904.06376); Kahan/pairwise summation", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06376", why_close: "Establishes low-precision-accumulator error as known prior art. The accumulator-overflow root cause is textbook; the packet correctly distinguishes this is summation-error lit, but it means the MECHANISM (fp16 sum blows past 65504 / loses precision over N) is fully known."}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM: Predominantly a NAMING/FRAMING delta, not a mechanism delta. Every constituent behavior is independently documented: (1) numpy float16-mean uses fp32 accumulator — in official docs; (2) numpy float16 reductions lose precision / "use float64" — official doc warning; (3) sklearn StandardScaler is fp64 — design fact; (4) pandas nanops preserves fp16 dtype causing overflow — verified in source + has historical issue-tracker precedent. The CLAIMED-NEW contribution is the *composition* — packaging these into a single "storage-dtype × tool-choice silent cross-tool divergence seam" with the three-tools/three-answers/no-error narrative. That is a genuinely useful re-framing, but it assembles known parts rather than revealing a new mechanism. The sharpest arguably-new element is pandas returning catastrophic `inf`/`nan` (not mere precision loss) at *moderate* N including unit-scale (the fp16 COUNT overflow at N>65504) — but that too is a direct consequence of the verified, documentable nanops dtype rule.

FATAL_COLLISIONS: None confirmed at the ≥2-independent-source bar — no single source documents the full three-tool simultaneous divergence as one phenomenon, so no KILL collision. (Partial collisions exist on each leg individually.)

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
- numpy.mean / numpy.sum official documentation float16 precision warning (REQUIRED — directly weakens the "neither endpoint predicts" argument; authors MUST address that the reduction side's own docs warn about float16).
- pandas nanops dtype-preservation behavior + any historical float16 sum/mean overflow issues (authors must do an exhaustive issue-tracker + changelog search, not just "current versions").
- arXiv:1904.06376 + Kahan/pairwise summation (already cited — keep).

RATIONALE: Not RED — no source documents the exact three-way cross-tool divergence together, and the in-source composition is honestly verified. But not GREEN either: this fails the stress-test A bar that yellowed CLAIM-0064 in spirit, because at least ONE endpoint *does* predict the hazard from its own side — numpy's official documentation explicitly warns that float16 reductions are precision-limited and to upcast. That is materially weaker than CLAIM-0059 (NFKC/NFC), where neither side's documentation predicts the 95% dup-escape. Here the mechanism is known textbook accumulator behavior (1904.06376), the per-tool behaviors are individually documented, and the residual novelty is the framing/packaging of a known failure mode into a "seam" — i.e., closer to "use float64 for reductions" folklore dressed as a cross-tool coupling than to a new mechanism. The pandas `inf`-at-moderate-N catastrophe is the one element that resists the folklore reduction and is worth preserving, but it is insufficient alone to clear the incremental-composition ceiling. I also flag that I could not complete a fully retrievable ≥2-source confirmation of zero pandas issue-tracker precedent within this review — the authors' "no GitHub issue" claim is scoped only to current versions and must be hardened. VOTE: yellow.
